# `zfetch`, a C program on libposix sockets, against the host-side test server
# (N4; `make test-net-posix`, which turns @PORT@ and the @SUM...@ checksums
# into numbers, as `make test-net-fetch` does). The program is /bin/zfetch of
# the disk, run through the broker; the shell gives it a connection to the host
# and port of the URL on its line (ADR-0224 item 2) at the NET slot.
! panicked
! first process ended
! tcptest: FAIL
< Type help to begin, or help tour.
< \r\n~>
# The lease takes a few hundred milliseconds under TCG.
> sleep 3\r
< \r\n~>
# The same page as `fetch`, byte for byte, by address.
> zfetch http://@HOST@:@PORT@/\r
< Zoraya test page

< fetched over HTTP, byte for byte

< \r\n~>
# Each framing, by checksum: the numbers `fetch --sum` is compared with.
> zfetch --sum http://@HOST@:@PORT@/pattern/1000000\r
< 1000000 bytes fnv1a @SUM1M@ in
< \r\n~>
> zfetch --sum http://@HOST@:@PORT@/close/70000\r
< 70000 bytes fnv1a @SUM70K@ in
< \r\n~>
# By name: getaddrinfo asks netd (net.hosts gives test.zoraya), and connect
# goes by the name it came from (INV-0225-2).
> +net test.zoraya:@PORT@ zfetch --sum http://test.zoraya:@PORT@/chunked/300000\r
< 300000 bytes fnv1a @SUM300K@ in
< \r\n~>
# More than the rings and the socket buffers hold.
> zfetch --sum http://@HOST@:@PORT@/pattern/4000000\r
< 4000000 bytes fnv1a @SUM4M@ in
< \r\n~>
# What goes wrong is said, and the exit status is 1.
> zfetch http://@HOST@:@PORT@/status/404\r
< zfetch: http://@HOST@:@PORT@/status/404: HTTP 404
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 1
< \r\n~>
> zfetch http://@HOST@:1/\r
< Connection refused
< \r\n~>
# A server that closes after the head: the page is cut, and the program says so.
> zfetch --sum http://@HOST@:@PORT@/reset\r
< the connection closed before the end of the page
< \r\n~>
# A URL from a value does not designate: the line is refused before it runs.
> set u http://@HOST@:@PORT@/\r
< \r\n~>
> zfetch $u\r
< from a value, outside the line's network grants
< \r\n~>
# Without the grant (INV-0225-1): the socket is made, and connect is refused.
> posixrun -m /bin=/bin:ro -m /home=/home -C /home /bin/zfetch http://@HOST@:@PORT@/\r
< zfetch: @HOST@: Permission denied
< posixrun: exit 1
< \r\n~>
# Plan part F7b (proposed ADR-0441): TCP listen, accept and connect over netd's
# loopback, one C program as server and client. The line is the grant: listen
# rules for its ports and a reach rule for 127.0.0.1 (ADR-0224's +listen and +net).
> +listen 9000-9009 +listen 50000-50010 +net 127.0.0.1 tcptest\r
< tcptest: basics ok
< tcptest: refusals ok
< tcptest: port zero ok
< tcptest: bulk ok
< tcptest: backlog bound and a closed listener with pending connections ok
< tcptest: all ok
< \r\n~>
# `grants` says what the line gives: the reach rule and the listen rules.
> grants +listen 9000-9009 +net 127.0.0.1 tcptest\r
< net: 127.0.0.1
< reach, any port
< net: ports 9000-9009
< listen (grant +listen)
< \r\n~>
# Without a listen word the program has no listener (INV-0440-1), and without a
# reach word for 127.0.0.1 it cannot connect to the loopback (INV-0440-2): each
# mode checks the refusal itself (EACCES) and prints its own ok line.
> +net 127.0.0.1 tcptest --no-listen\r
< tcptest: no listen grant ok
< \r\n~>
> +listen 9000-9009 tcptest --no-reach\r
< tcptest: no reach grant ok
< \r\n~>
# Plan part F9 (proposed ADR-0442, INV-0442-1 to -3): each room has its own loopback. Room ra serves
# A on ports 9100 and 9102; room rb serves B on 9100, the same port, which is no EADDRINUSE: the ports
# are per room. A connect reaches the listeners of its own room only, and every other answer is that of
# no listener (ECONNREFUSED), also past the 64 a queue holds, so that a room neither learns which ports
# another listens on nor fills its queue. The line's grant is the same in every room: `+net 127.0.0.1`.
> room new ra +w /home\r
< ra
> room new rb +w /home\r
< rb
> room new rc +w /home\r
< rc
> room ra\r
< ra ~>
> +listen 9100 tcptest --serve 9100 A &\r
< tcptest: serving A on 9100
> +listen 9102 tcptest --serve 9102 A &\r
< tcptest: serving A on 9102
> +net 127.0.0.1 tcptest --probe 9100 A 3\r
< tcptest: probe 9100 A x3 ok
< ra ~>
> room --leave\r
< \r\n~>
> room rb\r
< rb ~>
> +listen 9100 tcptest --serve 9100 B &\r
< tcptest: serving B on 9100
# Room rb reaches its own listener on 9100, not ra's, and finds nothing on 9102 (70 times: more than the queue).
> +net 127.0.0.1 tcptest --probe 9100 B 3\r
< tcptest: probe 9100 B x3 ok
< rb ~>
> +net 127.0.0.1 tcptest --probe 9102 none 70\r
< tcptest: probe 9102 none x70 ok
< rb ~>
# A room with no listener at all finds nothing on either port.
> room --leave\r
< \r\n~>
> room rc\r
< rc ~>
> +net 127.0.0.1 tcptest --probe 9100 none 3\r
< tcptest: probe 9100 none x3 ok
< rc ~>
> +net 127.0.0.1 tcptest --probe 9102 none 3\r
< tcptest: probe 9102 none x3 ok
< rc ~>
# Back in ra, its listeners answer as before.
> room --leave\r
< \r\n~>
> room ra\r
< ra ~>
> +net 127.0.0.1 tcptest --probe 9100 A 3\r
< tcptest: probe 9100 A x3 ok
< ra ~>
> +net 127.0.0.1 tcptest --probe 9102 A 3\r
< tcptest: probe 9102 A x3 ok
< ra ~>
# The home room has its own loopback too: none of the rooms' listeners.
> room --leave\r
< \r\n~>
> +net 127.0.0.1 tcptest --probe 9100 none 3\r
< tcptest: probe 9100 none x3 ok
< \r\n~>
> room remove ra --kill\r
< \r\n~>
> room remove rb --kill\r
< \r\n~>
> room remove rc --kill\r
< \r\n~>
x power off
> poweroff\r
